.TH APPATTIC 1 "2026-09-29" "appattic 3.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
appattic \- find leftover data, unused software, outdated packages, and disk usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B appattic
.RB [ --version ]
.RB [ --help ]
.RI [ command ]
.RB [ options ]
.br
.B appattic help
.RI [ command ]
.br
.B appattic disk
.RI [ PATH ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B appattic
scans the machine for data left behind by uninstalled apps, installed software
nothing has used, unused distro and language packages, packages with a newer
version, and folder sizes. The reporting commands delete nothing.
.B appattic update
is the one command that runs something, and only the named package upgrades
the scan found, never a full distro upgrade.
.PP
With no command it prints the full report. A scan is cached and reused for 24
hours; the status line on standard error names the time and points at
.BR \-\-fresh .
.PP
Progress and status go to standard error. Reports and
.B \-\-dry\-run
scripts go to standard output, so a report pipes cleanly:
.B appattic leftovers --top 10 | head
shows ten rows and no status lines.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B config
Print the settings file, its values, the path of the settings backup and of
the file a restore replaced (each with whether it is there), the scan cache
path, and the XDG paths this machine resolves, then exit.
Nothing is scanned, so it is safe to run anywhere.
.TP
.B report
Leftovers, stale software, outdated packages, and packages. The default.
.TP
.B leftovers
Only orphaned data and PATH overlays from uninstalled apps.
.TP
.B stale
Unused installed software, tiered KEEP, REVIEW, and REMOVE.
.TP
.B outdated
Installed packages with a newer version available.
.TP
.B packages
Distro orphans and language globals.
.TP
.BI "disk " [PATH ]
Folder sizes as a tree. PATH defaults to the home directory. PATH is a path:
it carries a separator, it is
.B .
or
.BR .. ,
or it is a name that starts with a dash and came behind
.BR \-\- .
.TP
.B erase
Delete the stored scan snapshot. It reads nothing first to decide, and it runs
before the settings are read, so it works on a machine whose
.I settings.json
no longer parses.
.TP
.BI "update " [ \-\-yes ]
Upgrade the named packages the scan found. It prompts on a terminal and exits
2 without one unless
.B \-\-yes
is given.
.PP
.B \-\-
ends the options, so a
.B disk
root that starts with a dash is passed as given:
.B appattic disk -- -backup
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-json " FILE"
Also write this command's result to FILE, which has to end in
.BR .json .
The report commands write the whole scan;
.B disk
writes the disk tree, and
.B config
and
.B erase
write what those commands reported.
.BR \-\-top ,
.BR \-\-category ,
.B \-\-leftovers\-only
and
.B \-\-stale\-only
shape what is printed, not what is written. A value that names no JSON file is a
usage error, so
.B appattic \-\-json erase
cannot write a file named
.B erase
instead of running the erase command.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-system
On report, leftovers, stale, outdated, packages, update, and config: include OS
system apps in the stale list. It turns the setting on for this run and cannot
turn it off when the settings file already has it true.
.TP
.B \-\-fresh
On report, leftovers, stale, outdated, packages, and update: ignore the
last-scan cache and scan now.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
On report, leftovers, stale, outdated, packages, and update: print the shell
script for the command without running it.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " N"
On report and leftovers: only the N largest. On disk: the N largest entries per
folder.
.TP
.BI \-\-category " CAT"
On report and leftovers: filter by category, substring match, repeatable. A
value that names a command is refused rather than taken, so
.B appattic \-\-category erase
reports the missing value and still reads
.B erase
as the command.
.TP
.B \-\-leftovers\-only
On report: skip stale, outdated, and packages.
.TP
.B \-\-stale\-only
On report: skip leftovers, outdated, and packages.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable ANSI color.
.B NO_COLOR
set to a non-empty value and
.B TERM=dumb
do the same.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-file\-systems
On disk: descend into other mounted devices.
.TP
.B \-\-allocated
On disk: print allocated blocks instead of apparent size.
.TP
.BR \-\-yes ", " \-y
On update: skip the prompt. Required without a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-v
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print the help and exit.
.B appattic help
is the same, and a command name after it is checked for a typo.
.PP
An option that names the commands it belongs to ("on report", "on disk", "on
update") is a usage error on every other command, so a flag that would be
ignored fails instead of running:
.B appattic stale --top 5
exits 2 rather than printing the full table.
.BR \-\-help " and " \-\-version
win over a usage error anywhere on the line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The run failed, or an update was cancelled.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: a bad command, option, or value; a disk root that is missing or is
not a directory; an update with no terminal and no
.BR \-\-yes ;
or a malformed
.IR settings.json .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
A non-empty value disables color, as
.B \-\-no\-color
does.
.TP
.B TERM
.B dumb
disables color.
.TP
.B COLORFGBG
.IR fg;bg .
The background decides the dark or light status colors.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Absolute data root, the parent of
.I appattic/settings.json
and
.IR appattic/last-scan.json .
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_CACHE_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME
Absolute configuration, cache, and state roots, scanned for leftovers.
.PP
Per the XDG Base Directory specification, an empty or relative XDG path is
ignored and the standard user default is used. The accepted values and their
effects are the ones
.B appattic config
prints.
.SH FILES
.PP
The two files below sit in one directory, which is
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/appattic
(default
.IR ~/.local/share/appattic )
on Linux and
.I ~/Library/Application Support/AppAttic
on macOS. It holds everything the app writes for the account.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/appattic/settings.json
.IR includeSystem ", " confirmDelete ", and the ignored leftover paths, which
must each be the full path a report prints. A missing file uses the defaults. A
malformed file is an error, and the run exits 2 without overwriting it.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/appattic/settings.json.bak
The settings file as it was before the last change, kept so an emptied or
mangled
.I settings.json
can be put back with a
.BR "cp" .
Nothing reads it while AppAttic runs.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/appattic/settings.json.bad
The settings file a restore replaced, kept so a restore that turns out to be
the wrong one is not a second loss. Written by a window's "Restore settings
from backup". Like
.IR settings.json.bak ,
nothing reads it while AppAttic runs, and
.B appattic erase
removes it. The procedure is
.IR docs/runbooks/state-recovery.md .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/appattic/last-scan.json
The scan cache, a full list of the app and leftover paths the last scan found
under your home directory. It is owner-only. A run that updates packages drops
it, so does
.BR "appattic erase" ,
and so does a run more than 24 hours after the one that wrote it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Ten largest leftovers:
.PP
.RS
.nf
appattic leftovers --top 10
.fi
.RE
.PP
Export the whole scan and keep the report on standard output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
appattic report --json "$HOME/scan.json" > report.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
The ten largest folders under /var, by allocated blocks:
.PP
.RS
.nf
appattic disk /var --top 10 --allocated
.fi
.RE
.PP
Filter by two categories:
.PP
.RS
.nf
appattic leftovers --category caches --category browser
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what an update would run, then run it unattended:
.PP
.RS
.nf
appattic update --dry-run
appattic update --yes
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
The project README and
.UR https://github.com/maci0/appattic
.UE
for the full scan description, and
.BR appattic\-qt (1)
for the Linux window.
